<AtlasLayout
  title="Editorial Philosophy"
  description="How to read the AI Evaluation Atlas: evidence-informed, transparent about interpretation, and written to encourage critical thinking rather than passive acceptance."
  currentPath="editorial-philosophy/"
>
  <header class="entry-header">
    <p class="kicker">How to read this Atlas</p>
    <h1>Editorial Philosophy</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="guide-prose" style="margin-top: 1.8rem;">
    <p>
      The AI Evaluation Atlas is an independent research project created to
      organize, explain, and encourage discussion about AI evaluation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Artificial intelligence evaluation is a rapidly evolving field. Many
      topics covered in this Atlas remain active areas of research where
      reasonable experts disagree about definitions, methods, evidence, and
      best practices.
    </p>
    <p>This Atlas attempts to distinguish between:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>formal standards;</li>
      <li>regulatory requirements;</li>
      <li>widely adopted practices;</li>
      <li>research conventions;</li>
      <li>emerging methods;</li>
      <li>the author’s own synthesis and interpretation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Whenever possible, entries are grounded in primary sources including
      research papers, technical documentation, standards, and official
      reports. Each entry shows its evidence class, its maturity, and the
      verification status of its sources, so you can see how firm the ground
      is beneath any particular claim.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, the organization of the Atlas, the proposed taxonomy, the
      comparisons between methods, and many of the broader interpretations
      represent my own effort to synthesize a fragmented field. They should
      not be interpreted as official guidance, universal consensus, or
      endorsement by any institution, employer, funding agency, publisher,
      standards body, or research organization.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the field genuinely disagrees, the Atlas says so: entries carry
      <em>Ongoing debate</em> sections that summarize competing positions
      rather than resolving them, and <em>Evidence boundaries</em> sections
      that state plainly which claims a method's results cannot carry. When
      the Atlas takes a position — and it does — the reasoning is on the
      page, where you can argue with it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I encourage readers to consult original sources, challenge the ideas
      presented here, compare alternative viewpoints, and develop their own
      understanding. The <a href={atlasUrl('methodology/')}>Methodology</a>
      page explains how entries are classified and verified, and every page
      links to a <a href={atlasUrl('guides/')}>guide</a> or source where the
      thinking can be checked.
    </p>
    <p>
      The purpose of the Atlas is not to provide final answers. Its purpose
      is to make AI evaluation easier to navigate and to encourage more
      rigorous thinking about what evaluation results actually mean.
    </p>

    <p class="epigraph">Every Atlas is a map, not the territory.</p>
  </div>
</AtlasLayout>
